<section aria-labelledby="problem">
  <h2 id="problem"><span class="eyebrow">01</span>The problem</h2>

  <p>I play adult league and drop-in hockey in Colorado. Finding ice time means checking a different website for every rink, logging in to see if there's room, and often still being told to call. I wanted one place where a skater can see every session they can actually book, right now.</p>

  <h3>The problems I set out to fix</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>You can't see what's available.</strong> Most rink systems hide open spots behind a login, or show them with no way to book.</li>
    <li><strong>There's no single source of truth.</strong> Rinks publish times that conflict with each other, then tell you to phone.</li>
    <li><strong>Casual skaters get left out.</strong> Drop-in is often members-only, and pricing assumes you're signing up for a season.</li>
    <li><strong>Team schedules depend on a volunteer.</strong> A captain retypes every ice time by hand, so games sit at "To be determined."</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout">
    <p class="callout__title">Problem statement</p>
    <p>Adult recreational skaters need to see which sessions they can actually book right now, because every system I studied hides availability behind a login or shows it with no way to act on it.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Who it's for</h3>
  <p>Adult drop-in and league players, parents booking for kids, and casual skaters who just want to get on the ice.</p>

  <h3>Constraints</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Solo project with no budget for paid tools or research participants.</li>
    <li>A small research sample, from my own network.</li>
    <li>No rink had signed on yet, so all rink data came from public listings.</li>
    <li>Waivers and legal text need a lawyer, so the design leaves room for them instead of writing them.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="research">
  <h2 id="research"><span class="eyebrow">02</span>Research</h2>

  <h3>What I wanted to learn</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Is there a real need, and who has it?</li>
    <li>How do people find out when they can skate, and how do they sign up?</li>
    <li>Where does the booking journey break?</li>
    <li>What do rinks need for this to work?</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How I found out, and why</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A hands-on study of real booking systems.</strong> I tried to book drop-in hockey on my phone at three Denver-area rinks, each on different software, and screenshotted every step. I wanted to see exactly where it breaks, not just hear about it.</li>
    <li><strong>Interviews</strong> with adult players, team captains, parents of youth skaters, and a casual skater, to hear the problem in their words.</li>
    <li><strong>A survey</strong> (8 responses) to check whether what I heard held up beyond the interviews.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>I started with only my own experience as a player, and my early personas were guesses. I needed real data before designing anything. Everyone I interviewed or surveyed agreed to take part and answered voluntarily. Names and identifying details are left out here.</p>

  {% figure "img/rinkside/as-is-study.png",
    "Summary of the as-is study: zero of three rinks let a motivated adult complete a booking on a phone. Three columns trace the steps at Edge Ice Arena, Apex Center and Big Bear, with dead ends marked in red.",
    "<strong>The as-is study:</strong> three rinks, three platforms, zero completed bookings on a phone.",
    "", true %}

  <h3>What hurt the most</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Availability is hidden or useless.</strong> Booking friction scored 3.1 out of 5 in the survey, the highest of anything I asked about. At one rink, booking a single session took 13 taps. Half the players I surveyed (4 of 8) had driven to a rink and found the session full, cancelled, or different from what they expected.</li>
    <li><strong>The schedule depends on a volunteer.</strong> Captains retype ice times by hand, so games show "To be determined."</li>
    <li><strong>Parents are already bridging apps by hand,</strong> copying schedules from the official platform into a team app.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>In their words</h3>
  <ul class="quote-list" role="list">
    <li><blockquote><p>"It would be great if I could see all the availability for local rinks in one place, like when I want to get movie tickets."</p><cite>Participant 2, adult league player</cite></blockquote></li>
    <li><blockquote><p>"Convenience with registration would supersede commute time."</p><cite>Parent 1</cite></blockquote></li>
    <li><blockquote><p>"Parents sometimes just copy all scheduling info into BenchApp for ease of use and common ground."</p><cite>Parent 3</cite></blockquote></li>
    <li><blockquote><p>"Almost didn't go — the sessions were full."</p><cite>Casual skater</cite></blockquote></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What the research told me</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>People want "movie tickets for ice."</strong> Four separate people described the same idea without being asked.</li>
    <li><strong>Friction scares off newcomers more than regulars.</strong> Committed players push through. First-timers give up. That makes it a growth problem, not just an annoyance.</li>
    <li><strong>Manual schedule entry is where teams fall apart.</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Competitive audit</h3>
  <p>I compared the booking platforms rinks use (CommunityPass, rec.us, EZFacility, SportsEngine, DaySmart Dash), BenchApp for team coordination, and similar apps like Volo Sports.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>None of them show sessions from more than one rink.</li>
    <li>Even the best one shows sessions you can't book, including ones that have already ended.</li>
    <li>No ice-specific app combines the official schedule with team attendance. Volo does, but only at its own venues.</li>
  </ul>
  {% figure "img/rinkside/competitive-matrix.png",
    "Feature matrix comparing seven competitors and Rinkside across 15 capabilities. Rinkside is the only column with every row checked, including aggregating multiple rinks and showing availability before login.",
    "<strong>Feature matrix.</strong> Everyone builds for the rink. Nobody builds for the skater.",
    "", true %}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="personas">
  <h2 id="personas"><span class="eyebrow">03</span>Personas &amp; journey map</h2>
  <p>Both personas are built from interviews. Their names and ages are made up. Their frustrations are real.</p>

  <div class="persona-grid">
    <article class="persona" aria-labelledby="p-easton">
      <h3 id="p-easton">Easton, 31</h3>
      <p>Plays in a Tuesday-night adult league, plus drop-in stick &amp; puck. Very comfortable with apps.</p>
      <h4>Goals</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>See his team's schedule and confirm he's coming, fast.</li>
        <li>Find last-minute drop-in when he wants extra ice.</li>
        <li>Not get surprised by a rink change or a Zamboni delay.</li>
      </ul>
      <h4>Frustrations</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>No way to know if a nearby drop-in has room without calling.</li>
        <li>His captain types every game in by hand, so future games say "To be determined."</li>
        <li>When the league changes a time, the team app still shows the old one.</li>
        <li>Teammates don't say if they're in until the last minute.</li>
      </ul>
      <h4>In his words</h4>
      <p>"It would be great if I could see all the availability for local rinks in one place, like when I want to get movie tickets." <span class="card__meta">(Participant 2)</span></p>
    </article>

    <article class="persona" aria-labelledby="p-maria">
      <h3 id="p-maria">Maria, 38</h3>
      <p>Works full time. Parent of one or two kids, ages 4–14, who skate at several rinks with different associations.</p>
      <h4>Goals</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Sign the kids up without hunting across organizations.</li>
        <li>Know right away if a session is full or cancelled.</li>
        <li>Keep track of who's skating when.</li>
      </ul>
      <h4>Frustrations</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Four or more separate organizations every season.</li>
        <li>Registration sites crash when spots open.</li>
        <li>Messages only reach one parent.</li>
        <li>Entering the same info into multiple apps.</li>
      </ul>
      <h4>In her words</h4>
      <p>"Convenience with registration would supersede commute time."</p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <h3>The journey today</h3>
  <p>A Google search lands on a rink's homepage, not its schedule. Then a login wall, a dead end, and "call to confirm." Most people give up or pick up the phone.</p>
