    "", true %}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="ideation">
  <h2 id="ideation"><span class="eyebrow">04</span>Ideation</h2>

  <h3>How might we…</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>…show a skater, on one screen, every session they can actually book today?</li>
    <li>…let someone try ice for the first time without a membership or a season commitment?</li>
    <li>…turn a 13-tap, three-website task into a few taps in one place?</li>
    <li>…make a team's schedule show up without anyone typing it in?</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Eight ways to answer one question</h3>
  <p>I sketched eight different main screens, all answering "when can I skate, and is there room?"</p>
  {% figure "img/rinkside/concept-sketches.png",
    "Eight wireframe concepts in a grid: chronological list (selected), map first, calendar grid (rejected), Tonight single answer, swipe deck (rejected), search first, rink first (rejected), and personalized feed (deferred).",
    "<strong>The eight concepts.</strong> The chronological list won.",
    "", true %}

  <h3>What I picked, and why</h3>
  <p>A <strong>chronological list of sessions</strong> as the backbone, with a "Tonight" card on top, a map you can switch to, and search always within reach. It answers the question directly, it's the easiest to scan, and it still works when a rink has only a few sessions listed.</p>

  <h3>What I dropped</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Calendar grid.</strong> It's what the current systems do, and it's dense and hard to read on a phone.</li>
    <li><strong>Swipe deck.</strong> Fun, but wrong for a quick, practical task.</li>
    <li><strong>Rink first.</strong> It repeats the mental model that's already failing people.</li>
    <li><strong>Replacing BenchApp outright.</strong> Teams already like it for chat and polls. Asking a whole roster to switch apps is a big ask, so Rinkside works alongside it.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="wireframes">
  <h2 id="wireframes"><span class="eyebrow">05</span>Wireframes &amp; prototype</h2>

  <h3>How the app is organized</h3>
  <p>Six tabs: Home, My Schedule, My Teams, Explore, Saved, and Profile. Every screen follows the same order: one hero item, a scannable list, then filters. I merged "Rinks" and "Activities" into one Explore tab with a toggle, because research showed people getting lost between competing tabs.</p>
