<section aria-labelledby="testing">
  <h2 id="testing"><span class="eyebrow">06</span>Usability testing</h2>

  <div class="callout callout--progress">
    <p class="callout__title">In progress</p>
    <p>I chose to build a working alpha first and test with the real app instead of a prototype. Two hockey teams are using it now, and I'm collecting their feedback over the next few weeks. I'll add the results here.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>The plan</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Watching people use it (3–5 people, about 30 minutes each).</strong> I give someone a task, like "find out if there's an open session tonight and how many spots are left," and ask them to think out loud while I watch where they hesitate.</li>
    <li><strong>A follow-up survey (8–10 people)</strong> with a standard usability score, so I can compare rounds.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The tasks check whether the problems from research are actually solved: Can you tell if a session has room within 10 seconds? Do you hit any dead ends? Can you join your team from an invite and answer In or Out? Booking, cancelling, and the goalie fee come later. The alpha sends people to the rink's own sign-up page until rinks join (see Final design).</p>
  <p>Feedback from the alpha comes in through the app's built-in feedback form.</p>

  <h3>Early signal</h3>
  <p>Before the alpha, two people tried the prototype informally. Both said, separately, that it was <strong>too dark</strong>. One also said it was "easy to use, very clear to navigate." Two people saying the same thing unprompted was enough for me to act on (see Iterations).</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="iterations">
  <h2 id="iterations"><span class="eyebrow">07</span>Iterations</h2>
  <p>So far, these changes came from research findings, an accessibility audit, and early feedback. Changes from the alpha testing will be added after it wraps up.</p>

  <ul class="change-list" role="list">
    <li>
      <p class="change__what">Checkout: goalies play free</p>
      <p>The first checkout wireframe treated everyone the same. I rebuilt it with an "I'm a goalie" toggle that waives the fee, and separate spot counts for skaters and goalies.</p>
      <p class="change__why">Why: research showed rinks let goalies skate free and track them separately.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="change__what">Check-in: from "show at the door" to self check-in</p>
      <p>I planned a confirmation screen to show staff at the door. I changed it to a check-in button in the app.</p>
      <p class="change__why">Why: none of the four real check-in processes I observed involved showing a phone.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="change__what">Notifications: let people choose</p>
      <p>I planned text messages only. I changed it so each person picks how they hear from the app.</p>
      <p class="change__why">Why: my third parent interview split the preference: two wanted texts, one wanted email.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="change__what">Theme: from dark-only to three themes</p>
      <p>The prototype was dark-only. The alpha ships with Light, Dark, and Warm themes, and each one is checked against WCAG contrast.</p>
      <p class="change__why">Why: both informal testers said it was too dark. Rinks are also bright, and a dark screen is harder to read there.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="change__what">Text contrast fixed across every screen</p>
      <p>Secondary text went from #3d6070 to #7aa3b8, raising contrast from 2.65:1 to 6.63:1.</p>
      <p class="change__why">Why: my own accessibility audit (next section).</p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="accessibility">
  <h2 id="accessibility"><span class="eyebrow">08</span>Accessibility</h2>
  <p>I'd assumed the prototype met WCAG AA. When I actually measured it, it didn't: the audit found four high-severity issues.</p>

  {% figure "img/rinkside/accessibility-audit.png",
    "Accessibility audit summary: 4 high, 2 medium and 1 low severity findings and 1 positive. Contrast bars show text-dim failing at 2.65:1 and 2.04:1 while other text passes; touch targets show the save button at about 26 by 26 pixels, failing the 44-pixel minimum.",
    "<strong>The audit.</strong> Every ratio computed from the real color values, not estimated.",
    "", true %}

  <ul class="change-list" role="list">
    <li>
      <p class="change__what">Readable secondary text</p>
      <p>Raised the dim text color from 2.65:1 to 6.63:1 contrast.</p>
      <p class="change__why">Helps: people with low vision, and anyone reading their phone under bright rink lights.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="change__what">Visible keyboard focus</p>
      <p>Added a clear focus outline to every control.</p>
      <p class="change__why">Helps: people who navigate with a keyboard or switch device.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="change__what">Bigger tap targets</p>
      <p>The save (heart) button was about 26×26 px, well under the 44×44 px minimum. The audit flagged it for more padding.</p>
      <p class="change__why">Helps: people with limited dexterity, and anyone tapping with gloves on.</p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>In the alpha</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Three themes, all checked.</strong> Every text color in Light, Dark, and Warm measures at least 4.5:1 on every background. The check also caught a power-play badge at 1.9:1, which I fixed.</li>
    <li><strong>Every control has a name.</strong> All 117 tappable elements have a role and a label for screen readers, and errors and confirmations like "Link copied" are read out loud.</li>
    <li><strong>Big tap targets.</strong> Buttons and inputs are at least 48 points tall, and small icon buttons get a bigger tap area.</li>
    <li><strong>Text grows with the phone's settings,</strong> and team banners grow instead of cutting off text.</li>
    <li><strong>No gesture-only actions.</strong> The one long-press (reporting a chat message) is also available as a screen reader action.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Helps: people with low vision, people who use VoiceOver or TalkBack, people with limited dexterity, and anyone squinting at their phone under rink lights.</p>

  <h3>Still to do</h3>
  <p>All of this comes from reviewing the code, not from testing on a device. Next is a real VoiceOver and TalkBack pass, a check at the largest text size and in landscape, and a color-blindness check on the In / Maybe / Out colors.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="final">
  <h2 id="final"><span class="eyebrow">09</span>Final design: the alpha</h2>

  <p>I'm launching Rinkside in four steps, and the alpha is step one. Without partner rinks there's nothing to book or pay for inside the app yet, so step one focuses on what skaters can use today: finding sessions near them and keeping track of their team.</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Skater app (now):</strong> find sessions near you and run your team. Sign-ups still happen on each rink's own website.</li>
    <li><strong>Free founders' version of <a href="/design/rinkside-b2b/">Rinkside for rinks</a>:</strong> partner rinks publish sessions once. In-app booking and payments start here, with a small transaction fee.</li>
    <li><strong>Tournaments and associations</strong> on both the skater and rink sides.</li>
    <li><strong>Premium subscriptions</strong> for rinks.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>What changed from the prototype</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Booking hands off to the rink.</strong> The prototype's checkout, fees, waitlist, and refunds wait for step two. In the alpha you tap through to the rink's sign-up page, then add the session to your schedule.</li>
    <li><strong>The team side grew.</strong> Captains invite teammates with a text link. Players answer In or Out. Schedules import from a league link, calendar, or screenshot (read by the Claude API). Scores, standings, and stats fill in from the league's site, and players get an alert when the league moves a game.</li>
    <li><strong>Sessions from 73 rinks,</strong> pulled from the rinks' own public calendars and websites.</li>
    <li><strong>Three themes</strong> (Light, Dark, Warm), in response to "too dark."</li>
  </ul>

  <ul class="compare" role="list">
    <li>{% figure "img/rinkside/app-home-mobile.png", "Alpha Home screen: greeting, the user's team with its next game, a My schedule row of upcoming games with In and Out buttons, and rinks near me.", "<strong>Home.</strong> Your team's next game, with In / Out right there.", "phone" %}</li>
    <li>{% figure "img/rinkside/app-explore-mobile.png", "Alpha Explore screen: Activities and Rinks tabs, session-type filters, a Google map of rink pins around Littleton, Colorado, and the closest rink.", "<strong>Explore.</strong> Rinks and sessions near you, on a map or a list.", "phone" %}</li>
    <li>{% figure "img/rinkside/app-team-mobile.png", "Alpha team page for Ice Wolves: a Manage Team button for the captain, record 2-0-1, 1st in the standings, standings and stats links, and the roster.", "<strong>Team.</strong> Record, standings, and stats fill in on their own.", "phone" %}</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>On a computer</h3>
  <p>The same app runs on the web, in a centered column on wide screens.</p>
  {% figure "img/rinkside/app-home-desktop.png", "The alpha Home screen in a desktop browser: the same team card, schedule row with In and Out buttons, and rinks section in a centered column with the tab bar along the bottom.", "<strong>Home</strong> on desktop.", "", true %}
  {% figure "img/rinkside/app-explore-desktop.png", "The alpha Explore screen in a desktop browser: filters, the rink map, and the closest rinks in a centered column.", "<strong>Explore</strong> on desktop.", "", true %}

  <h3>The main flow: joining your team</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>The captain taps "Text it to teammates." The text has an invite link.</li>
    <li>A teammate taps the link. It opens the app, or offers the download.</li>
    <li>They sign up with email, Google, Apple, or Facebook. The invite is remembered through sign-up.</li>
    <li>The team opens with the code filled in. They pick player or goalie and join.</li>
    <li>They see the next game and tap In or Out.</li>
    <li>On game day they get a reminder, plus an alert if the league moves the game.</li>
    <li>After the game, the score, standings, and their stats fill in from the league's site.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The clickable prototype still shows the full booking flow planned for step two.</p>
  <p><a class="button" href="https://beaudoi1.github.io/rinkside-prototype/rinkside-welcome.html">Try the prototype</a></p>

  <h3>Design choices, and why</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Every nearby session in one list or map,</strong> because people want "movie tickets for ice."</li>
    <li><strong>In / Out right on the Home screen,</strong> because captains spend their week chasing replies.</li>
    <li><strong>Schedules imported, not typed,</strong> because a volunteer retyping the league schedule is where teams fall apart.</li>
    <li><strong>Alerts when the league changes a game,</strong> because stale times in team apps were a real frustration.</li>
    <li><strong>One Explore tab with a toggle,</strong> because separate tabs confused people.</li>
    <li><strong>Booking stays on the rink's site for now,</strong> so I'm not showing prices or spots I can't guarantee until rinks are partners.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Back to the problems</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Can't see what's available:</strong> Explore shows sessions from 73 rinks in one place, on a map or a list. Live spot counts need partner rinks (step two).</li>
    <li><strong>No single source of truth:</strong> one list across rinks, pulled from their own calendars until rinks publish directly through Rinkside.</li>
    <li><strong>Casual skaters left out:</strong> anyone can find public skate, stick &amp; puck, and drop-in near them without joining anything. Booking without a membership comes with in-app booking in step two.</li>
    <li><strong>Schedules depend on a volunteer:</strong> import once from a link or screenshot, and scores and standings fill in on their own.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Design system</h3>
  <p>The prototype's tokens and components became the starting point for the app's three themes.</p>
