<section aria-labelledby="takeaways">
  <h2 id="takeaways"><span class="eyebrow">10</span>Takeaways</h2>

  <h3>What I learned</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Research can overturn your own assumptions. I was sure check-in meant showing a phone at the door. None of the four real check-ins I watched worked that way.</li>
    <li>Rink staff and players describe the same problem in completely different words.</li>
    <li>Friction keeps newcomers out much more than it bothers regulars.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What surprised me</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Parents are already doing this job by hand, copying schedules between apps.</li>
    <li>My own prototype failed my accessibility check. I'd assumed it passed.</li>
    <li>I misread one interview: what I took as a request was really a complaint, and it flipped a finding.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What I'd do differently</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Test with users before starting the build. I started engineering alongside testing to get to a real alpha sooner, and I'd lock the design first next time.</li>
    <li>Base every persona on evidence from the start.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What's next</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Finish alpha testing with the two teams and add the results here.</li>
    <li>Run the VoiceOver, TalkBack, and color-blindness checks on real devices.</li>
    <li>Step two: bring rinks on board with <a href="/design/rinkside-b2b/">Rinkside for rinks</a>, and turn on in-app booking.</li>
  </ul>

